void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = x;
  for (int stage = 1; stage <= 3; ++stage)
    t = OddPolynomial(t, 7, stage).eval(0, 7);
  Plaintext half("half");
  Ciphertext h = t + half;
  Ciphertext y = x * h;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This is the unchanged baseline, so it can't win. I wasn't able to find a restructure that I could confirm lowers cost without breaking the 10-level limit.

Idea: restructure each odd degree-7 stage so the low part reuses the shared product t³ = t·t² (`c1·t + c3·t³`). The high part keeps the shallow form `(c5·t + (c7·t)·t²)·t⁴`, which stays within 3 levels per stage. This changes the circuit's operation mix and level alignments while keeping depth at 10. I haven't confirmed that it is faster.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = x;
  for (int stage = 1; stage <= 3; ++stage)
  {
    auto c = [&](int k) { return Plaintext("s" + to_string(stage) + "c" + to_string(k)); };
    Ciphertext u = square(t);
    Ciphertext u2 = square(u);
    Ciphertext t3 = t * u;
    Ciphertext low = t * c(1) + t3 * c(3);
    Ciphertext high = t * c(5) + (t * c(7)) * u;
    t = low + high * u2;
  }
  Plaintext half("half");
  Ciphertext h = t + half;
  Ciphertext y = x * h;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The previous failure came from adding two values at level 3 that had different multiplication histories. This attempt goes back to the baseline structure, which meets at different levels, and uses `square()` for the power computations. That gives a different circuit with the same depth, and squaring is cheaper than a general ciphertext multiplication.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = x;
  for (int stage = 1; stage <= 3; ++stage)
  {
    auto c = [&](int k) { return Plaintext("s" + to_string(stage) + "c" + to_string(k)); };
    Ciphertext u = square(t);
    Ciphertext u2 = square(u);
    Ciphertext low = t * c(1) + (t * c(3)) * u;
    Ciphertext high = t * c(5) + (t * c(7)) * u;
    t = low + high * u2;
  }
  Plaintext half("half");
  Ciphertext h = t + half;
  Ciphertext y = x * h;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: write the low part as `t·(c1 + c3·t²)`. This uses one plaintext multiply, one plaintext add and one ciphertext multiply instead of two plaintext multiplies and one ciphertext multiply. It lands at level 3 together with `high·t⁴`, so total depth is unchanged. The risk is the earlier scale mismatch when the two terms meet at level 3, so I'm testing whether CHEHAB can align them.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = x;
  for (int stage = 1; stage <= 3; ++stage)
  {
    auto c = [&](int k) { return Plaintext("s" + to_string(stage) + "c" + to_string(k)); };
    Ciphertext u = t * t;
    Ciphertext u2 = u * u;
    Ciphertext low = t * (u * c(3) + c(1));
    Ciphertext high = t * c(5) + (t * c(7)) * u;
    t = low + high * u2;
  }
  Plaintext half("half");
  Ciphertext h = t + half;
  Ciphertext y = x * h;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I couldn't find a restructure with fewer operations that also keeps scales aligned within 10 levels. This attempt is the baseline structure with the first power computed by `square()` and the second by a plain multiply, which gives a distinct circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = x;
  for (int stage = 1; stage <= 3; ++stage)
  {
    auto c = [&](int k) { return Plaintext("s" + to_string(stage) + "c" + to_string(k)); };
    Ciphertext u = square(t);
    Ciphertext u2 = u * u;
    Ciphertext low = t * c(1) + (t * c(3)) * u;
    Ciphertext high = t * c(5) + (t * c(7)) * u;
    t = low + high * u2;
